 ## Overview
-Remote Rendering Client/Server Architecture separates the volume rendering engine into a server and a client connected via a network, allowing rendering computation to be performed on a remote machine while the user interacts locally [S1][S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of limited local hardware performance or data accessibility by centralizing data storage and rendering power on a server, while the client handles user interface and display [S1][S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from client-side compositing approaches (like Visapult) by performing all rendering and compositing remotely, resulting in a "thin client" that only receives final composited RGB images [S2, unofficial].
+Remote Rendering Client/Server Architecture separates the volume rendering engine into a server and a client connected via a network, allowing rendering computation to be performed on a remote machine while the user interacts locally [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of limited local hardware performance or data accessibility by centralizing data storage and rendering power on a server, while the client handles user interface and display [S1][S2, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from client-side compositing approaches (like Visapult) by performing all rendering and compositing remotely, resulting in a "thin client" that only receives final composited RGB images [S2, unofficial].
 
